# A named Structure is called by `S()` -- one P0 Frame with `S: ()` -- as by the bare atom S (book §9 after 55c1abc: a
# callable, an ordinary named Structure included, is called, never assigned; steps/named-struct-exec.md §12: its
# procedure over itself).  Counter's body runs at each call: the declaration stores its initializer again, then the
# statement -- Counter\n reads 1 after each call, though 5 was written before it, from the root (walked) and from bump
# (natively).  Before the ticket `S()` assigned a new empty Structure to S with admission to S's type, which () fails:
# `implements`.  Entry 7.
Counter:
    size_t: n 0U
    n: n + 1U
return
fn: bump () size_t
    Counter()
    size_t: v 9U
    v: Counter\n
return: v
Counter\n: 5U
Counter()
size_t: a 9U
a: Counter\n
Counter\n: 5U
Counter: ()
size_t: b 9U
b: Counter\n
Counter\n: 5U
size_t: c 9U
c: bump()
int: ok 0
if: a = 1U && b = 1U && c = 1U
    ok: 7
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
